  "reasoning": "The patient presents with systemic fatigue, altered taste, a scratchy throat, fever ('heat'), muscle pain, and whole-body cramps. These symptoms collectively point toward an acute systemic viral infection. Conditions like common cold or allergies typically do not cause severe muscle pain or whole-body cramps. Malaria usually presents with cyclic chills and sweats, while typhoid focuses more on gastrointestinal symptoms. Dengue is well-known for causing high fever, profound fatigue, severe myalgia and arthralgia (often described as 'breakbone fever' leading to cramps), and can present with throat irritation and altered taste due to systemic inflammation and dehydration. Therefore, dengue is the most consistent diagnosis among the options provided.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]dengue[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}
